There’s a bigger infinity hiding in all the ways you could wire the rungs.

Our mirror helix has one exact pairing. Now keep both strands fixed and group their points into pairs of positions. In each pair, you can leave the two rungs alone or swap their partners. There are \beth_\omega independent places to make that choice, so you get

2^{\beth_\omega}=\beth_{\omega+1}

distinct wiring patterns on the same fixed strands.

One more twist. If every rung has to preserve the exact mirror order, there’s only one possible pairing. Let the rungs cross however they want and there’s a strictly larger infinity of pairings.

So that’s my next roll. \beth_{\omega+1}, made entirely out of choices about what connects to what. 🎲